<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Hanukkah War, 2018</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;SPAN lang=EN&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;The Hanukkah War 0f 2018, V3.0&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Al Sandrik (BeirutDude)&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;The Time Is: 2 December 2018, 12:00 UTC/14:00 Tel Aviv Time&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Sunrise, Tel Aviv: 06:24&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Sunset, Tel Aviv: 16:46 &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Israeli, Lebanon and Syria&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Playable as Israel/IDF only&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Up to a 4 Days Duration &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;HAPPY HANUKKAH! &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Situation Briefing: Over the first 8 months of 2018 the Syrian Arab Army reestablished their authority over the western and southwestern portions of the county with major Russian, Iranian and Hezbollah assistance. The Iranian assistance did not come without major strings attached as from January 2013 onward the Iranian Revolutionary Guards Corps (IRGC) established a more significant presence and, more significantly, during 2018 a growing capability to strike directly at Israel. In April and May Israeli airstrikes hit many Iranian IRGC Quds Force (special forces) targets as well as units of the IRGC and Syrian Air Defense Force. By summer an uneasy peace had fallen over the region with IAF aircraft testing Syrian defenses and conducting recon over the Bekaa/Beqaa Valley of Lebanon. The Israeli Defense Minister had publically made it known to both Russia and Iran that any attempt to improve the Syrian Air Defense Forces capabilities would be resisted with direct air strikes and in particular any attempt to bring S-300 Batteries in to Southwestern Syria would directly lead to war. Neither nation immediately made any attempt to do so with Russia content to defend their naval base at Tartus and Iran concentrating on defeating the Syrian Rebels that summer. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;The "November Crisis" began on November 20th when American satellite imagery was passed to Israel showing the Iranians attempting, once again, to establish an Intermediate Ballistic Missile base near the 47th Syrian Brigade Bases in the vicinity of Homs. This was the same gambit which lead to the IAF launching their April and May aerial offensive and once again, led to an attempted IAF strike on the 23rd. The raid turned into a fiasco when bad weather closed in and a Syrian SA-200 SAM caused damage to a returning F-15I. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Late in November the weather remained bad as a series of low pressure systems moved in from the Med maintaining low ceilings and heavy rain. Iran took advantage of this opportunity to establish an S-300 battery southwest of Damascus in a position where its radars and missiles could threaten most of Israel’s air space. United States demanded its removal in public declarations and in the U.N. Security Council with the latter vetoed by Russia on the 30th On the evening of the 31st, the Israeli cabinet met behind closed doors and voted to take action. By late in the day on December 1st The Israeli mobilization had brought the Golan defenses up to division level (36th the "Ga'ash" Armored Udga) with yet another division (319th "Ha-Mapatz" Reserve Mechanized Udga/Division) opposite Hezbollah positions in southern Lebanon. That night the Iranian Defense Minister announced that any concentration of IAF aircraft over Israel proper which appeared to be offensive in nature would be struck without warning, "If Israel sees no need to respect Arab borders striking our forces at will, then we will see no need to respect their airspace to defend ours." The streets in several Arab nations overflowed with celebration about the new confrontation and, more significantly, the growing crisis led to the closure of all civil airfields in the region and the rerouting of civilian traffic over the Med. The spark had found its fuse… &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;As the Chief of the General Staff you have been instructed to remove the threat to Israel from both the S-300 SAMs and Iranian and Syrian IRBM presence while minimizing Israeli Losses. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Designers Notes: You don’t receive any points for destroying Russian, Hezbollah or most Syrian units (Syrian IRBMs/SSMs and vessels are the exception). Look closely at the IDF missiles along the borders as they have interesting capabilities. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Your real targets are the Iranian forces, the Syrian IRBMs/SSMs and Syrian navy, but you will have to deal with Syrian Air Defense Forces to get at them. Hezbollah and Russia do not begin as belligerents with Hezbollah unfriendly and Russia neutral toward Israel. Be careful not to engage the Russians as you are subject to collective responsibility and their air defenses are formidable! Hezbollah may enter the conflict if you use Lebanese territory and if they do their extensive SAM capabilities could be arrayed against Israeli aircraft. The scenario starts at 10 a.m. UTC (12 p.m. Tel Aviv) time with load outs. A CAP mission has been established but for the most part they are left up to you. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Losses to IAF units will cost you heavily and it is fine line between a victory and perceived defeat. Remember Middle East conflicts are as much a political exercise as a military action, so you have to play to two masters, the military situation and an Israeli public with little tolerance for large losses. Plan well! I have stationed the IAF units at their actual bases. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Important: Part of the scenario design is to either have the player consider and achive a Time on Target (TOT) attack from seperate bases OR through rebasing [during missions] or ferry missions stage the aircraft as desired for future opertions. Your IAF counterpart would face the same challange. There are loadouts (but not a lot, also part of the challange) at the auxillary fields. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Default settings are for ACTIVE Radar and OECM due to the shear number of ground radars in use. Suggest you check the ENCOM on your missions if yu want Passive.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;P.S. Yes, Israel has nukes but they are only there as potential targets.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Both an INI and Delta file are provided for rebuilds&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
